And then from that, we go back to the demand function,

10
01:20:38,440 --> 01:20:40,360
and we find out p.

11
01:20:40,360 --> 01:20:42,160
And so p for the demand function is just

12
01:20:42,160 --> 01:20:46,880
going to be a times n plus 1 over 4n.

13
01:20:51,280 --> 01:20:53,760
And then we just go and multiply these things.

14
01:20:53,760 --> 01:20:57,360
We're going to multiply pq, p times the market price

15
01:20:57,360 --> 01:21:01,240
is little q, to find out what your defection problems would be.

16
01:21:01,240 --> 01:21:03,880
And it comes out to that thing in the notes, a squared times n

17
01:21:03,880 --> 01:21:06,960
plus 1 squared all over 16bn squared.

18
01:21:06,960 --> 01:21:10,680
And then for collusion, now we can plug everything in.

19
01:21:10,680 --> 01:21:14,360
So collusion will be sustainable if this thing here, right,

20
01:21:14,360 --> 01:21:17,720
if we go and plug that in for pi m,

21
01:21:17,720 --> 01:21:21,200
and we go and we plug that thing that I told you in for pi d,

22
01:21:21,200 --> 01:21:25,040
and we go and put this in for pi c.

23
01:21:25,040 --> 01:21:28,440
So we plug in every single profit function

24
01:21:28,440 --> 01:21:30,480
to find out the profits.

25
01:21:30,480 --> 01:21:35,520
And then we get that giant mess in part j,

26
01:21:35,520 --> 01:21:37,520
which I will not read, because it's a book.

27
01:21:37,520 --> 01:21:40,040
And if you look at that, you may get into a panic,

28
01:21:40,040 --> 01:21:43,480
because it appears to be a fourth degree equation, right.

29
01:21:43,480 --> 01:21:47,040
And who here knows how to solve a fourth degree equation?

30
01:21:47,040 --> 01:21:47,760
Yes.

31
01:21:47,760 --> 01:21:49,720
There is a book that explains it.

32
01:21:49,720 --> 01:21:51,400
The formula is about two pages long.

33
01:21:51,400 --> 01:21:53,000
I'm not reading it.

34
01:21:53,000 --> 01:21:58,880
All right, so economists never solve fourth degree equations.

35
01:21:58,880 --> 01:22:01,240
I have seen economists go and change a model just

36
01:22:01,240 --> 01:22:03,560
to make sure they wouldn't need to have a fourth degree

37
01:22:03,560 --> 01:22:08,400
equation solved, because they'd go, well, why not?

38
01:22:08,400 --> 01:22:10,240
Of course, maybe the world does require you

39
01:22:10,240 --> 01:22:11,840
to solve fourth degree equations.

40
01:22:11,840 --> 01:22:13,500
I think this is a case where you really

41
01:22:13,500 --> 01:22:16,920
do have to give a sociological slash economic theory of people

42
01:22:16,920 --> 01:22:20,520
saying, look, people in my professional subculture

43
01:22:20,520 --> 01:22:22,120
don't solve fourth degree equations.

44
01:22:22,120 --> 01:22:23,280
It's not done.

45
01:22:23,280 --> 01:22:26,120
So if I go and do it, I'm not going to get published.

46
01:22:26,120 --> 01:22:29,560
And then I will not get desirable material possessions

47
01:22:29,560 --> 01:22:30,800
and status.

48
01:22:30,800 --> 01:22:32,800
So I'm not going to change the model

49
01:22:32,800 --> 01:22:34,760
to make sure that it doesn't work out

50
01:22:34,760 --> 01:22:37,080
into a fourth degree model.

51
01:22:37,080 --> 01:22:39,160
This is true for theory.

52
01:22:39,160 --> 01:22:40,700
In empirics, on the other hand, there

53
01:22:40,700 --> 01:22:42,600
are some cases where you're required

54
01:22:42,600 --> 01:22:45,240
to do a fourth degree equation.

55
01:22:45,240 --> 01:22:48,560
20 years ago, there was a paper saying that experience

56
01:22:48,560 --> 01:22:50,680
goes and follows a quartic.

57
01:22:50,680 --> 01:22:53,480
And ever since, technically common papers

58
01:22:53,480 --> 01:22:56,040
put in experience, experience squared, experience cubed,

59
01:22:56,040 --> 01:22:59,680
experience to the fourth power as control variables.

60
01:22:59,680 --> 01:23:01,280
And if you ever wondered, when someone

61
01:23:01,280 --> 01:23:04,400
says I'm going to put in a quartic in experience, why?

62
01:23:04,400 --> 01:23:07,000
Because of this paper 20 years ago said that you should do it,

63
01:23:07,000 --> 01:23:08,600
and now everybody does it.

64
01:23:08,600 --> 01:23:13,920
Of course, it's much easier to go and do that in an OLS

65
01:23:13,920 --> 01:23:15,840
or something than it is to go and get

66
01:23:15,840 --> 01:23:18,600
the formula for getting an analytical solution to an

67
01:23:18,600 --> 01:23:20,720
equation.

68
01:23:20,720 --> 01:23:22,360
But in any case, if you look at this

69
01:23:22,360 --> 01:23:24,280
and think you've got a fourth degree equation,

70
01:23:24,280 --> 01:23:27,480
you are wrong, actually, because we're not solving for n.

71
01:23:27,480 --> 01:23:29,200
We're solving for beta.

72
01:23:29,200 --> 01:23:33,080
So phew, we don't actually need the equation for a fourth

73
01:23:33,080 --> 01:23:35,040
degree equation.

74
01:23:35,040 --> 01:23:37,920
We can just go and use our regular simple math

75
01:23:37,920 --> 01:23:41,040
that we already know, and then we can memorize.

76
01:23:41,040 --> 01:23:43,560
And we get this value of beta, which I will say

77
01:23:43,560 --> 01:23:44,640
is totally counterintuitive.

78
01:23:44,640 --> 01:23:45,880
But if you put in some numbers, then you'll

79
01:23:45,880 --> 01:23:47,520
see the numbers make sense.

80
01:23:47,520 --> 01:23:50,760
If I have n is 2, then beta is 1 by 3.

81
01:23:50,760 --> 01:23:54,160
That's the first value.

82
01:23:54,160 --> 01:23:58,360
So that is Carnot collusion for you.

83
01:23:58,360 --> 01:23:59,880
Now, after doing all this math, it's

84
01:23:59,880 --> 01:24:02,440
worth pointing out that all the math ignores a bunch

85
01:24:02,440 --> 01:24:05,920
of super important things.

86
01:24:05,920 --> 01:24:09,000
And why would the math ignore super important things?

87
01:24:09,000 --> 01:24:11,880
Well, because first of all, the math

88
01:24:11,880 --> 01:24:14,400
is not empirical in the first place.

89
01:24:14,400 --> 01:24:17,840
So it isn't really designed to go and assess

90
01:24:17,840 --> 01:24:19,320
what's important in the real world.

91
01:24:19,320 --> 01:24:20,940
And then secondly, the important things

92
01:24:20,940 --> 01:24:24,720
might actually not require a lot of mathematical ability

93
01:24:24,720 --> 01:24:25,240
to discuss.

94
01:24:25,240 --> 01:24:28,720
And so if you think that possibly economists sometimes

95
01:24:28,720 --> 01:24:32,160
put in extra math just to signal how smart they are in papers,

96
01:24:32,160 --> 01:24:36,560
anyone here holds this cynical and despicable theory?

97
01:24:36,560 --> 01:24:41,480
That I have heard famous economists admit in public,

98
01:24:41,480 --> 01:24:43,520
or the Ashenfelter would sometimes say,

99
01:24:43,520 --> 01:24:45,860
and now this is the part where I added in some needless

100
01:24:45,860 --> 01:24:48,320
complexity to get published.

101
01:24:48,320 --> 01:24:52,240
So I suppose you could be lying about deliberately putting

102
01:24:52,240 --> 01:24:54,080
in some useless math into a paper.

103
01:24:54,080 --> 01:24:57,800
But you seem totally honest to me.

104
01:24:57,800 --> 01:25:00,520
So the main thing that all of this ignores

105
01:25:00,520 --> 01:25:03,640
is new entry, which almost any empirical discussion

106
01:25:03,640 --> 01:25:06,680
of collusion will say is incredibly important.

107
01:25:06,680 --> 01:25:09,120
Because it's one thing just to get

108
01:25:09,120 --> 01:25:11,800
unanimity, which is hard in the first place.

109
01:25:11,800 --> 01:25:13,880
So if there's five firms in the industry,

110
01:25:13,880 --> 01:25:17,840
you have to get all five of them to agree to the collusion, which

111
01:25:17,840 --> 01:25:19,280
is hard enough.

112
01:25:19,280 --> 01:25:22,400
So I always give my students the challenge of,

113
01:25:22,400 --> 01:25:24,320
you guys can all collude in the amount of work

114
01:25:24,320 --> 01:25:26,520
that you do, since this class is great and on a curve.

115
01:25:26,520 --> 01:25:28,840
You can all do less work, have more fun,

116
01:25:28,840 --> 01:25:30,440
and then get the same grade distribution

117
01:25:30,440 --> 01:25:32,160
as you would have otherwise.

118
01:25:32,160 --> 01:25:34,960
I've never seen even a hint that students are successfully

119
01:25:34,960 --> 01:25:36,280
colluding.

120
01:25:36,280 --> 01:25:39,160
Once I did teach a class with only four students in it,

121
01:25:39,160 --> 01:25:41,960
and then I was like, maybe I should not

122
01:25:41,960 --> 01:25:45,000
taunt them in this way, because maybe they would.

123
01:25:45,000 --> 01:25:47,400
All I can say is when I looked at the exams,

124
01:25:47,400 --> 01:25:48,920
at least one student didn't collude.

125
01:25:48,920 --> 01:25:53,560
One student had a score that exceeded that

126
01:25:53,560 --> 01:25:54,360
of the other three.

127
01:25:54,360 --> 01:25:56,800
Maybe they colluded, and he drank them.

128
01:25:56,800 --> 01:25:58,640
But in any case, there's the general problem

129
01:25:58,640 --> 01:26:00,760
of getting unanimity among all firms,

130
01:26:00,760 --> 01:26:03,880
because if all firms but one fail to cooperate,

131
01:26:03,880 --> 01:26:06,000
then it's very hard to make it work.

132
01:26:06,000 --> 01:26:08,200
But on top of it, even if you get all existing firms

133
01:26:08,200 --> 01:26:10,200
in the industry, there's a problem of new entry.

134
01:26:10,200 --> 01:26:12,880
Because if you do manage to get profits up and usually high,

135
01:26:12,880 --> 01:26:14,600
the normal thing for other outside firms

136
01:26:14,600 --> 01:26:16,600
to want to do is say, look, there's high profits.

137
01:26:16,600 --> 01:26:17,480
I want to try.

138
01:26:17,480 --> 01:26:19,400
Again, in principle, you could go and predate on them

139
01:26:19,400 --> 01:26:20,400
as soon as they show up.

140
01:26:20,400 --> 01:26:22,560
But then you need to have a really effective cartel,

141
01:26:22,560 --> 01:26:24,320
a cartel that not only includes everyone,

142
01:26:24,320 --> 01:26:28,040
but where someone snaps his fingers, and then new entry.

143
01:26:28,040 --> 01:26:30,040
Let's all unite against them now.

144
01:26:30,040 --> 01:26:31,160
And then, OK, they're gone.

145
01:26:31,160 --> 01:26:33,540
All right, now go back and resume our regularly scheduled

146
01:26:33,540 --> 01:26:34,040
collusion.

147
01:26:34,040 --> 01:26:38,920
You could try that, but in the real world, not too likely.

148
01:26:38,920 --> 01:26:42,320
If you go in, so very often, the most interesting,

149
01:26:42,320 --> 01:26:43,840
plain ways to learn about collusion

150
01:26:43,840 --> 01:26:46,040
is to go and read the history, economic history

151
01:26:46,040 --> 01:26:47,400
before antitrust.

152
01:26:47,400 --> 01:26:49,360
Because back in those days, there

153
01:26:49,360 --> 01:26:51,640
would be very little dispute about what the facts are.

154
01:26:51,640 --> 01:26:54,240
Today, if you want to collude, you need to do it secretly.

155
01:26:54,240 --> 01:26:55,740
And if people are doing it secretly,

156
01:26:55,740 --> 01:26:57,840
then there can always be some people saying,

157
01:26:57,840 --> 01:26:59,800
it happened in secret, and other people saying,

158
01:26:59,800 --> 01:27:01,880
no, it didn't happen at all.

159
01:27:01,880 --> 01:27:04,000
But in the 19th century, you can often go and see

160
01:27:04,000 --> 01:27:05,800
people in the newspaper saying, we're

161
01:27:05,800 --> 01:27:09,760
trying to go and reduce leather production to raise prices.

162
01:27:09,760 --> 01:27:10,960
And you can see what happened.

163
01:27:10,960 --> 01:27:14,760
And all the problems that you hear about in a good econ

164
01:27:14,760 --> 01:27:19,600
class are very evident in 19th century cartels, where, yes,

165
01:27:19,600 --> 01:27:21,400
first of all, there's the trouble of getting

166
01:27:21,400 --> 01:27:22,840
all the firms in the industry in.

167
01:27:22,840 --> 01:27:24,920
And then there's the trouble of new entry.

168
01:27:24,920 --> 01:27:27,420
And one that's one particularly interesting kind of new entry

169
01:27:27,420 --> 01:27:30,640
is that very often, you would go and buy someone out,

170
01:27:30,640 --> 01:27:33,140
and then they would just start, take the money you gave them,

171
01:27:33,140 --> 01:27:35,440
and build a new firm, and then start competing with you.

172
01:27:35,440 --> 01:27:38,520
Usually, there would be an early version of a non-compete clause

173
01:27:38,520 --> 01:27:40,060
where they would promise to not enter

174
01:27:40,060 --> 01:27:41,560
the industry for five years.

175
01:27:41,560 --> 01:27:43,400
But again, in the real world, it would often

176
01:27:43,400 --> 01:27:47,800
take three or four years just to get the cartel working

177
01:27:47,800 --> 01:27:48,880
and functional.

178
01:27:48,880 --> 01:27:52,640
And then a guy says, well, one year from today,

179
01:27:52,640 --> 01:27:55,440
I'm opening the doors for my new firm.

180
01:27:55,440 --> 01:27:59,040
So they had a great deal of trouble.

181
01:27:59,040 --> 01:28:00,440
So questions about collusion?

182
01:28:00,440 --> 01:28:03,240
Anyone wondering about collusion?

183
01:28:03,240 --> 01:28:04,560
And moving on.

184
01:28:04,560 --> 01:28:06,280
So public goods and game theory.

185
01:28:06,280 --> 01:28:08,440
So I assume everyone here has heard about public goods

186
01:28:08,440 --> 01:28:09,800
and externalities at some point.

187
01:28:09,800 --> 01:28:12,440
All right, good.

188
01:28:12,440 --> 01:28:15,720
So there's a lot of treatments that emphasize non-rivalry,

189
01:28:15,720 --> 01:28:18,160
although actually, non-rivalry intrinsically

190
01:28:18,160 --> 01:28:20,160
has absolutely nothing at all to do with this.

191
01:28:20,160 --> 01:28:23,280
You can have a totally private good with non-rivalry,

192
01:28:23,280 --> 01:28:26,000
as long as you don't have perfect competition.

193
01:28:26,000 --> 01:28:28,680
So right here in this Cournot model we talked about,

194
01:28:28,680 --> 01:28:29,880
we got zero marginal cost.

195
01:28:29,880 --> 01:28:33,040
So is there non-rivalry?

196
01:28:33,040 --> 01:28:36,200
You could produce one more unit without actually going

197
01:28:36,200 --> 01:28:38,360
and taking away from anybody else.

198
01:28:38,360 --> 01:28:42,000
And there are many firms that we think of as private

199
01:28:42,000 --> 01:28:44,520
where actually they're clearly non-rival.

200
01:28:44,520 --> 01:28:48,280
So go to a movie theater during the matinee, a bunch of MTCs,

201
01:28:48,280 --> 01:28:49,960
so non-rival.

202
01:28:49,960 --> 01:28:51,540
In fact, the more you think about it,

203
01:28:51,540 --> 01:28:53,480
the harder it is to find rivalry.

204
01:28:53,480 --> 01:28:56,280
Because suppose you go to a restaurant

205
01:28:56,280 --> 01:28:57,280
and you order some food.

206
01:28:57,280 --> 01:28:58,580
You might see the food's rival.

207
01:28:58,580 --> 01:28:59,840
It's like, well, wait a second.

208
01:28:59,840 --> 01:29:02,400
What was going to happen to that food if you didn't order it?

209
01:29:02,400 --> 01:29:04,480
A lot of times it was just going to get thrown out

210
01:29:04,480 --> 01:29:05,440
at the end of the day.

211
01:29:05,440 --> 01:29:09,160
So maybe the food was rival too.

212
01:29:09,160 --> 01:29:09,660
Awesome.

213
01:29:09,660 --> 01:29:12,200
You look puzzled.

214
01:29:12,200 --> 01:29:17,960
Very often a restaurant will make 100 meals.

215
01:29:17,960 --> 01:29:20,560
And then if 90 people show up, what

216
01:29:20,560 --> 01:29:22,280
do they do with the other 10 meals?

217
01:29:22,280 --> 01:29:23,640
They throw them away.

218
01:29:23,640 --> 01:29:26,360
So then if person 91 comes up, the marginal cost

219
01:29:26,360 --> 01:29:30,800
of getting that food is basically zero.

220
01:29:30,800 --> 01:29:33,000
So anyway, a lot of treatments talk about non-rivalry.

221
01:29:33,000 --> 01:29:35,440
But really, non-excludability is what

222
01:29:35,440 --> 01:29:38,880
makes these cases special and interesting.

223
01:29:38,880 --> 01:29:40,700
And again, with non-excludability,

224
01:29:40,700 --> 01:29:43,320
there is this basic logic of selfishness,

225
01:29:43,320 --> 01:29:45,520
which is by definition of this non-rivalry,

226
01:29:45,520 --> 01:29:47,840
that there's no, or assuming non-excludability,

227
01:29:47,840 --> 01:29:50,920
there's no feasible way to exclude non-payers.

228
01:29:50,920 --> 01:29:54,480
And then if there's no way to exclude non-payers

229
01:29:54,480 --> 01:29:58,960
and people are selfish, what will the selfish people do?

230
01:29:58,960 --> 01:29:59,460
Yes.

231
01:29:59,460 --> 01:30:02,640
So wait, can I get the benefit whether or not I pay?

232
01:30:02,640 --> 01:30:03,320
Yes.

233
01:30:03,320 --> 01:30:04,440
And I'm selfish?

234
01:30:04,440 --> 01:30:05,440
Yes.

235
01:30:05,440 --> 01:30:06,880
Then I guess I won't pay.

236
01:30:06,880 --> 01:30:08,560
I'll just keep the money.

237
01:30:08,560 --> 01:30:10,880
And again, imagine that you actually pass a hat.

238
01:30:10,880 --> 01:30:12,360
And you invite people, put in money

239
01:30:12,360 --> 01:30:14,600
to contribute to however much you want.

240
01:30:14,600 --> 01:30:16,800
And the selfish people all put in zero.

241
01:30:16,800 --> 01:30:18,640
And then you go to a producer and say,

242
01:30:18,640 --> 01:30:20,640
would you produce this public good in exchange

243
01:30:20,640 --> 01:30:22,920
for the contents of this hat?

244
01:30:22,920 --> 01:30:24,840
And then he says, show me what's in the hat.

245
01:30:24,840 --> 01:30:26,280
Sorry, there's nothing in the hat.

246
01:30:26,280 --> 01:30:27,440
All right, so that's the end of it.

247
01:30:27,440 --> 01:30:28,600
There's no one to pay for it.

248
01:30:28,600 --> 01:30:30,800
Well, selfish producers are not going to provide it.

249
01:30:33,000 --> 01:30:35,840
So this does lead us to standard diagrams

250
01:30:35,840 --> 01:30:38,880
for externalities of public goods.

251
01:30:44,880 --> 01:30:47,440
So positive and negative externalities are really easy.

252
01:30:47,440 --> 01:30:49,100
There's actually two ways you can do it,

253
01:30:49,100 --> 01:30:53,120
but I'm going to show you the easiest way of all I do.

254
01:30:53,120 --> 01:30:58,120
So this is just quantity, price, supply to mend.

255
01:30:58,120 --> 01:31:01,160
And then the idea of a positive externality

256
01:31:01,160 --> 01:31:05,240
is that there's some additional benefits that go to bystanders,

257
01:31:05,240 --> 01:31:08,040
people who don't have to pay to get them.

258
01:31:08,040 --> 01:31:10,600
And so we can just add a social benefits curve

259
01:31:10,600 --> 01:31:13,280
on top, which includes the benefits to the paying

260
01:31:13,280 --> 01:31:17,880
customers plus all the benefits to the bystanders.

261
01:31:17,880 --> 01:31:20,280
All the benefits to the bystanders.

262
01:31:20,280 --> 01:31:24,240
And then, since people in markets are selfish,

263
01:31:24,240 --> 01:31:27,320
where do they produce given all these bystanders around

264
01:31:27,320 --> 01:31:29,360
who enjoy what they do for free?

265
01:31:29,360 --> 01:31:30,760
Intersection of supply and demand.

266
01:31:30,760 --> 01:31:32,080
Intersection of supply and demand, yes.

267
01:31:32,080 --> 01:31:33,760
So wait, so you're telling me there's a bunch of bystanders

268
01:31:33,760 --> 01:31:36,640
and they don't pay us anything, but there's a lot of them.

