Right? Like, none of us have clothes that have, like, a covering of our face.

25
01:81:34,000 --> 01:81:36,000
Right? So, like, none of us are wearing masks.

26
01:81:36,000 --> 01:81:38,000
Like, why not?

27
01:81:38,000 --> 01:81:40,000
So, see, there's comfort.

28
01:81:40,000 --> 01:81:41,000
Mask week is next week.

29
01:81:41,000 --> 01:81:42,000
Yes.

30
01:81:42,000 --> 01:81:44,000
Right? You have a set of, like, you know, if you were...

31
01:81:44,000 --> 01:81:46,000
Imagine you were a society where everybody wore masks.

32
01:81:46,000 --> 01:81:49,000
You would probably wear one, too, just to fit in.

33
01:81:49,000 --> 01:81:51,000
Right? And there are many other things like this.

34
01:81:51,000 --> 01:81:58,000
So, for example, how hard should you encourage a guest at your home to eat food?

35
01:81:58,000 --> 01:82:01,000
So, there is the American norm where you say, would you like some food?

36
01:82:01,000 --> 01:82:03,000
And if the person says yes, you give it to them.

37
01:82:03,000 --> 01:82:05,000
And if they say no, then you don't give it to them.

38
01:82:05,000 --> 01:82:07,000
And that's it.

39
01:82:07,000 --> 01:82:10,000
But on the other hand, if you're in a Romanian home, they have a totally different rule.

40
01:82:10,000 --> 01:82:15,000
And the rule is they offer you food and you say no, and then they insist.

41
01:82:15,000 --> 01:82:20,000
And if you don't want it, then you say no again, and they will then insist even more strongly.

42
01:82:20,000 --> 01:82:23,000
And then they may try to actually put it on your plate when you don't want it.

43
01:82:23,000 --> 01:82:25,000
And then you may need to move your plate.

44
01:82:25,000 --> 01:82:29,000
And in this system, if you...

45
01:82:29,000 --> 01:82:30,000
Like, it can be rude just to...

46
01:82:30,000 --> 01:82:34,000
It can be rude not to refuse even when you do want.

47
01:82:34,000 --> 01:82:35,000
Right?

48
01:82:35,000 --> 01:82:38,000
And both systems can work well if everybody accepts the norm.

49
01:82:38,000 --> 01:82:42,000
So, in the U.S., if both people accept the norm of I offer, and if you want it, you say yes.

50
01:82:42,000 --> 01:82:44,000
And if you don't, you say no, and that's it.

51
01:82:44,000 --> 01:82:49,000
In that case, the guest gets the food and the host feels like they've been a good host.

52
01:82:49,000 --> 01:82:56,000
On the other hand, if you have two Romanians together, then the Romanian host offers and the guest politely refuses,

53
01:82:56,000 --> 01:83:02,000
and then the host offers again, and then the guest may then accept at that point, and then everybody's happy.

54
01:83:02,000 --> 01:83:10,000
But if you mix up the American with a Romanian, then you can have the unpleasant situation where one person keeps pushing

55
01:83:10,000 --> 01:83:13,000
and the other person doesn't know how to not receive food they don't want.

56
01:83:13,000 --> 01:83:20,000
And then there's a breakdown and people get more upset or upset.

57
01:83:20,000 --> 01:83:21,000
All right.

58
01:83:21,000 --> 01:83:22,000
So, lots of examples.

59
01:83:22,000 --> 01:83:24,000
So, like many other cultural examples you can think of.

60
01:83:24,000 --> 01:83:25,000
Yes?

61
01:83:25,000 --> 01:83:27,000
The song, Baby, It's Cold Outside.

62
01:83:27,000 --> 01:83:28,000
No, don't...

63
01:83:28,000 --> 01:83:33,000
Christmas song called, Baby, It's Cold Outside, where this guy's trying to get her to stay over,

64
01:83:33,000 --> 01:83:35,000
and she also wants to stay over, and that's what the song is about.

65
01:83:35,000 --> 01:83:36,000
Ah, okay.

66
01:83:36,000 --> 01:83:40,000
And she keeps making up reasons, like, why she should go.

67
01:83:40,000 --> 01:83:41,000
And it's basically the same thing.

68
01:83:41,000 --> 01:83:42,000
Yeah, yeah.

69
01:83:42,000 --> 01:83:45,000
So, again, like, some cultures are very straightforward, others are not.

70
01:83:45,000 --> 01:83:50,000
I remember philosopher John Searle was telling me that he said, like, you know, so he was American,

71
01:83:50,000 --> 01:83:52,000
but he said, you know, be at international philosophy conferences,

72
01:83:52,000 --> 01:83:59,000
and you would see two extremely different cultural norms about how much personal space a person needs to have a conversation.

73
01:83:59,000 --> 01:84:05,000
So, like, the British norm, like, you need, like, a good four-foot gap to have a proper conversation.

74
01:84:05,000 --> 01:84:09,000
And the Italian norm, maximum, like, six inches.

75
01:84:09,000 --> 01:84:17,000
So, you know, he said you could actually look and you could see, like, the Italians almost literally chasing the Brits around the room.

76
01:84:17,000 --> 01:84:23,000
Because the Italian keeps trying to get close enough to talk, and the Brit keeps trying to get far enough away to have a conversation.

77
01:84:23,000 --> 01:84:24,000
Right?

78
01:84:24,000 --> 01:84:28,000
And, again, as long as both sides have the same cultural norm, then there'd be no problem.

79
01:84:28,000 --> 01:84:34,000
But when you have two incompatible norms, then you do have this difficulty.

80
01:84:34,000 --> 01:84:40,000
So, coordination needs have been invoked to explain culture, and a lot of this makes a lot of sense.

81
01:84:40,000 --> 01:84:42,000
Okay.

82
01:84:42,000 --> 01:84:44,000
So, coordination needs have been used for technology.

83
01:84:44,000 --> 01:84:46,000
Remember I mentioned this path-dependence literature?

84
01:84:46,000 --> 01:84:51,000
So, again, the idea is that there are some technologies where the reason to have it is that other people have it.

85
01:84:51,000 --> 01:84:59,000
So, when I was first teaching this class, there were two competing standards for the next-generation DVDs.

86
01:84:59,000 --> 01:85:00,000
Right?

87
01:85:00,000 --> 01:85:05,000
So, there's the one that won, which is Blu-rays, and then there's a totally different standard, which had a better name.

88
01:85:05,000 --> 01:85:07,000
HD-DVD.

89
01:85:07,000 --> 01:85:08,000
Very intuitive.

90
01:85:08,000 --> 01:85:09,000
Right?

91
01:85:09,000 --> 01:85:11,000
It's like high-definition DVD.

92
01:85:11,000 --> 01:85:12,000
But Blu-ray actually won.

93
01:85:12,000 --> 01:85:13,000
Right?

94
01:85:13,000 --> 01:85:18,000
But there was a period, about two years, when they were both fighting to become the winner.

95
01:85:18,000 --> 01:85:22,000
And during this period, there were an enormous number of people, including me, who just didn't buy either,

96
01:85:22,000 --> 01:85:28,000
because we were waiting for coordination to kick in and wanted to dig over so we wouldn't need to go and get two players,

97
01:85:28,000 --> 01:85:33,000
or make the wrong bet and then be stuck with a player that didn't work, which a bunch of people did.

98
01:85:33,000 --> 01:85:36,000
I made the wrong bet.

99
01:85:36,000 --> 01:85:38,000
You must be getting one at a very young age.

100
01:85:38,000 --> 01:85:39,000
Yes.

101
01:85:39,000 --> 01:85:40,000
Ah, all right.

102
01:85:40,000 --> 01:85:43,000
So, anyway, that's one case.

103
01:85:43,000 --> 01:85:46,000
Or, again, think about credit cards of a certain size.

104
01:85:46,000 --> 01:85:52,000
Would any bank want to go and come up with a double-sized credit card?

105
01:85:52,000 --> 01:85:53,000
Right?

106
01:85:53,000 --> 01:85:56,000
Here, we have our new double-sized credit cards.

107
01:85:56,000 --> 01:85:58,000
Right? They're great.

108
01:85:58,000 --> 01:86:06,000
And, again, even if it was better, who would want one, given that it wouldn't fit in anyone else's machines?

109
01:86:06,000 --> 01:86:08,000
You wouldn't swipe it anywhere.

110
01:86:08,000 --> 01:86:10,000
It wouldn't fit in wallets.

111
01:86:10,000 --> 01:86:16,000
So this is one where you want to go and have the same dimensions for your technology as everybody else.

112
01:86:16,000 --> 01:86:18,000
Regardless of anyone.

113
01:86:18,000 --> 01:86:24,000
Even if the other one had some extra merits, you still would have a reason to want to just have the same technology as everybody else.

114
01:86:24,000 --> 01:86:27,000
It sounds like something Steve Jobs would do.

115
01:86:27,000 --> 01:86:28,000
Yeah, maybe.

116
01:86:28,000 --> 01:86:34,000
So, of course, if you're a really big player, you might think, I'm so important in this world that I can change the world.

117
01:86:34,000 --> 01:86:37,000
I can get everyone to switch over to my new thing.

118
01:86:37,000 --> 01:86:41,000
So, in that case, maybe you could.

119
01:86:41,000 --> 01:86:42,000
All right.

120
01:86:42,000 --> 01:86:43,000
So, anyway, technology reviews.

121
01:86:43,000 --> 01:86:45,000
And then location is another great example.

122
01:86:45,000 --> 01:86:49,000
So Paul Krugman is still bitter that his work on location hasn't really caught on.

123
01:86:49,000 --> 01:86:55,000
I think he probably has hundreds of citations, but Paul Krugman is a high stander for how successful his work has to be.

124
01:86:55,000 --> 01:86:57,000
But it comes down to this.

125
01:86:57,000 --> 01:86:59,000
Why do you want to use Manhattan in Manhattan?

126
01:86:59,000 --> 01:87:02,000
Isn't that a stupid place to have Manhattan?

127
01:87:02,000 --> 01:87:06,000
It gets cold and you're on an island, so it's inconvenient to get in and out.

128
01:87:06,000 --> 01:87:09,000
And the land supply is very low.

129
01:87:09,000 --> 01:87:16,000
Why not just take everyone on Manhattan, everything on Manhattan, and move it to Florida?

130
01:87:16,000 --> 01:87:19,000
Like a nice, unoccupied area of Florida.

131
01:87:19,000 --> 01:87:22,000
Which, of course, is what Walt Disney did for the Magic Kingdom.

132
01:87:22,000 --> 01:87:26,000
Because he got a giant unoccupied area and he just built his dream world there.

133
01:87:26,000 --> 01:87:35,000
But why not just scoop up the entire area of Manhattan and move everyone to another area where there would no longer be any bridges or tunnels to worry about,

134
01:87:35,000 --> 01:87:39,000
where you could expand out easily, and you'd have way better weather.

135
01:87:39,000 --> 01:87:44,000
And the coordination answer is, well, you go first.

136
01:87:44,000 --> 01:87:51,000
If you're already in Manhattan, you have a good job in Manhattan, and you say, fine, I'm going to move to the middle of nowhere in Florida,

137
01:87:51,000 --> 01:87:56,000
what happens is not that you get to have all the benefits of Manhattan combined with all the other great stuff,

138
01:87:56,000 --> 01:88:01,000
what happens is that you lose it all because unless everyone moves as a group, you have a problem.

139
01:88:01,000 --> 01:88:08,000
Now, if you know the history of cities, what are the best predictors of where a city today will generally be?

140
01:88:08,000 --> 01:88:11,000
Where the same city was 100 years ago.

141
01:88:11,000 --> 01:88:15,000
And why did cities generally get placed where they are historically?

142
01:88:15,000 --> 01:88:19,000
Water. Yes, water is the main reason.

143
01:88:19,000 --> 01:88:22,000
So you'll see all these cities are either by coast or by major rivers.

144
01:88:22,000 --> 01:88:29,000
Even though now we have a bunch of improvements in transportation, which make it much better to have cities in other places,

145
01:88:29,000 --> 01:88:32,000
but yet still on a likelihood, likelihood.

146
01:88:32,000 --> 01:88:34,000
So what does it suggest?

147
01:88:34,000 --> 01:88:38,000
So I think actually 90% of the U.S. population is within 100 miles of the border even.

148
01:88:38,000 --> 01:88:41,000
And most of the other, so an enormous amount of that is just from the coast.

149
01:88:41,000 --> 01:88:44,000
So, anyway, that is the reason.

150
01:88:44,000 --> 01:88:51,000
So Krugman likes to talk about, like, why is it that almost all carbon production happens in Atlanta, like outside of Atlanta?

151
01:88:51,000 --> 01:88:56,000
And there's no particular reason based upon physical inputs or crops or anything else,

152
01:88:56,000 --> 01:89:01,000
just that, well, there's some, like, since everybody else is doing it there, you want to do it there too.

153
01:89:01,000 --> 01:89:07,000
And of course, like, why go and put your startup in Silicon Valley when the real estate costs are astronomical?

154
01:89:07,000 --> 01:89:11,000
It's like, well, because there's a whole lot of other qualified people that are already there.

155
01:89:11,000 --> 01:89:16,000
So if I set my business up there, super high cost, but there's a big pool of qualified applicants.

156
01:89:16,000 --> 01:89:18,000
So that makes it better.

157
01:89:18,000 --> 01:89:38,000
And we will stop here.


